On Aug 31, 8:22=A0pm, Prometheus77 <aarons...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Also, would I be better off with Grayswandir and its massive
> backstabbing bonus, or Magicbane and its curse resistance?

Magicbane's curse resistance is not absolute and should not be
absolutely relied upon:  it *can* become cursed, and you *do*
need to maintain a way to uncurse it.

With that said, it is nonetheless useful.  I once wished for it
in a Tourist game, and I did not regret the choice.  It's also
good for engraving, although by the time you get the castle
wand you probably have wands of fire already anyway.

Regarding Grayswandir, I only know what's in the Wiki,
since in all my Lawful games to date, if I've made it to the
Castle, I already had Excalibur.  (This may change when
I start putting more effort into lawful roles that don't start
with a longsword and can't get skill in it, e.g., Caveman.
Although, with the artifact's to-hit bonus, even a Caveman
might be able to make good use of Excalibur, if he can
find a longsword to dip.  Perhaps I should start a separate
thread on that question.)

Grayswander (unlike Excalibur) _can_ be used by cross-
aligned players who can stand the blasting damage, but
I haven't personally tried it.  I imagine I will eventually.
It would be most tempting for a chaotic, since there are
few really awesome chaotically-aligned artifacts (unless
you count ones that are awesomely more trouble than
they're worth, notably, StormBringer).  Neutrals have to
balance the desire to wish for a cross-aligned weapon
artifact against their numerous excellent co-aligned
non-weapon artifact wishing options, not least the Eye
of Teleport At Will As Often As You Want.  With the
co-aligned artifacts a neutral player can potentially
get from sacrifice being better than the chaotic ones
and perhaps good enough in many cases, it's tempting
to spend the artifact wish on something else and take
your chances on sacrificing for a weapon.  That goes
double for (non-wizard) spell casters, who can make
*really* good use of the Eye.


